Subject: [PATCH] EE thread (FPS round 3): direct calls, the scratchpad inline; call-site and register write-back options

The game thread (EE) is the frame-rate limit in levels (TODO 94). Each change runs the same guest
code in the same order with the same EE clock: a scripted cold boot into Amazon and the fight
route (aim, walk, fire, melee, car), both all in turbo with VIF1 on the EE thread, give the same
game state on every frame as before (8,018 and 19,068 game ticks, on the test PC, the other PC
and locally), and the native renderer's shots are pixel-identical.

- Direct calls (ps2xRecomp config `direct_calls`): a `jal` to a known function becomes
  runtime->dispatchGuestCall(rdram, ctx, target, source, fallthrough, PS2_CALL_TARGET(target)) -
  the generated code reads the target's entry of the function table itself (one load from a fixed
  place) instead of the dispatcher's lookup (thread-local table, hashing, misses into the 10 MB
  table). The entry IS what the lookup returns (a replaced function's replacement too: replacing
  writes the entry and bumps the lookup's generation), so the same function is entered, after
  dispatchGuestBranch's checkpoint at the same point; the rest of a call (PS2Runtime::enterCall /
  afterCall) is dispatchGuestBranch's code, shared. An empty entry, or PS2X_DIRECT_CALLS=0 at run
  time, takes dispatchGuestBranch.
- Direct call sites (config `direct_call_sites`, with `direct_calls`; off): the call itself made at
  the jal's site when the entry is still the generated function - Ps2CallKnown: callBegin, a
  direct call, callEnd (what dispatchGuestCall does before and after its call; the level the
  dispatcher's scope gives back when an EeDispatcherTransfer leaves it is set back to 0 where the
  scheduler catches one, PS2Runtime::dispatchUnwound). Measured ~2% slower than dispatchGuestCall
  on both workers (two out-of-line calls a call instead of one); PS2X_DIRECT_CALLS=1 makes such a
  build take dispatchGuestCall.
- The scratchpad inline: READn/WRITEn in the generated code read and write the scratchpad's
  memory straight after the main-RAM test (an aligned access in its 16 KB), before the
  special-address tests and the call - PS2Runtime::Load*/Store* did exactly that after them
  (Ps2ScratchpadRead, storeScratchpadFast; a 32-bit store still drains the completed DMA
  handlers after it, as Store32 did). PS2Runtime::Load8..128 take the same way first (the
  translator's constant-address scratchpad loads call them: 514 sites, which went through
  PS2Memory::read* and translateAddress). PS2X_INLINE_SCRATCHPAD=0: as before.
- Register write-back (ps2xRecomp config `register_write_back`, off): a register written to its
  C++ local only, the ones written since stored back before a call, a jump to another function,
  a syscall, a return or a loop's checkpoint (inside its `if`), with the set at a label taken
  from the gotos to it (a fixpoint). Measured SLOWER (+3% a frame with the label states, +29%
  with every written register at a label): the deferred stores keep the locals live across the
  function and MSVC spills them, while it already drops most of the stores the write-through
  form makes. Kept as an option for another compiler.
- ps2_register_cache.h: RC_PUT_* / RC_WB for that option.

Not in this patch (the port's overrides.toml): minimal_pc_stores, which existed and is now on.

On a GTX 1080 / Ryzen 5 5600 test PC, Amazon from its first gate, the user's settings at 1080 lines,
120 held, game ticks 8600-13000: see TODO 94's round-3 paragraph.
---
